<h1>GNR638: Coding Assignment 2</h1>
<h3>Pre-trained CNN Representation Transfer and Robustness Analysis</h3>

<p>
    This repository contains the implementation and technical report for an investigative study into the transferability, efficiency, and robustness of pre-trained Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs). We evaluate three distinct architectures—<b>ResNet-50</b>, <b>EfficientNet-B0</b>, and <b>DenseNet-121</b>—on the <b>Aerial Images Dataset (AID)</b>[cite: 152].
</p>

<hr>

<h2>🛰️ Project Overview</h2>
<p>
    The objective of this assignment is to systematically analyze representation transfer, fine-tuning strategies, and model behavior under distribution shifts[cite: 151, 153]. We explore how architectural design influences performance across five experimental scenarios[cite: 153, 157]:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Scenario 1: Linear Probe Transfer</b> – Evaluating frozen backbones as feature extractors[cite: 186].</li>
    <li><b>Scenario 2: Fine-Tuning Strategies</b> – Comparing full fine-tuning, last-block adaptation, and selective 20% unfreezing[cite: 262, 267].</li>
    <li><b>Scenario 3: Few-Shot Learning</b> – Testing data efficiency at 100%, 20%, and 5% training regimes[cite: 399].</li>
    <li><b>Scenario 4: Corruption Robustness</b> – Assessing sensitivity to Gaussian noise, motion blur, and brightness shifts[cite: 431, 432].</li>
    <li><b>Scenario 5: Layer-Wise Probing</b> – Examining semantic abstraction evolution across network depth[cite: 452, 453].</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>📊 Key Findings</h2>
<ul>
    <li><b>Best Extractor:</b> <b>DenseNet-121</b> achieved the highest validation accuracy (<b>91.02%</b>) in linear probing, demonstrating superior inductive bias for aerial imagery[cite: 191, 243].</li>
    <li><b>Robustness Leader:</b> <b>ResNet-50</b> showed the most stable behavior under input corruptions like Gaussian noise, maintaining higher accuracy compared to other models at stronger corruption levels[cite: 436, 448].</li>
    <li><b>The Robustness Trade-off:</b> An unexpected finding revealed that while DenseNet-121 performed best on clean data, its dense feature reuse mechanism acted as an "error amplifier," making it the most sensitive to noise[cite: 630, 632].</li>
    <li><b>Few-Shot Efficiency:</b> DenseNet-121 maintained the strongest performance in extreme low-data regimes (5% data), leveraging its dense connectivity to preserve learned representations[cite: 413, 601].</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>🛠️ Tech Stack & Requirements</h2>
<ul>
    <li><b>Frontend API:</b> Python 3.12 [cite: 127, 647]</li>
    <li><b>Core Libraries:</b> PyTorch 2.2.0, TIMM (HuggingFace), Matplotlib, Scikit-learn [cite: 647]</li>
    <li><b>Hardware:</b> NVIDIA T4 GPU (16 GB VRAM) via Google Colab [cite: 643, 644]</li>
</ul>

